# Characters and strings

## Stack representation

Preferred form: **address/length pair** `( c-addr u )` for `type`, `compare`, `evaluate`.

Counted string `( c-addr )` — first byte is length; `count ( c-addr1 -- c-addr2 u )` unpacks to a pair.

## Character memory

Chars are one byte in memory, one cell on the stack:

```forth
create hello
  char h c,  char a c,  char l c,  char l c,  char o c,
hello 5 type
```

Use `c@` / `c!` / `char+` / `chars` for byte buffers — not `@` / `!` unless aligned.

## String literals

| Task | Idiom |
|------|--------|
| Pair inside `: … ;` | `s" hello"` (permanent while definition exists) |
| Interpreter one-liner | `s" hello" type cr` |
| Counted literal | `c" hello"` |
| Escapes | `s\" line\n"` |
| Compile-time string | `s" hello" sliteral` |

**Space required:** `s" hello"` — `s"` is a word; `s"hello"` fails.

## s" lifetime

Interpreted `s"`: buffer is ephemeral — overwritten by the next interpret-time `s"`. Inside a colon definition, compiled `s"` strings persist for the life of that definition.

```forth
: greet ( -- )  s" world" type cr ;
```

Prefer `s"` over non-ANS `"hello"` for portability. Do not use C NUL-terminated strings unless binding C — see `forth-c-bindings.mdc`.

## Extended characters (Unicode)

`xc` = one extended character (one cell). Buffer `( xc-addr u )` — **`u` is byte length (pchars)**, not xchar count.

```forth
: xtype ( xc-addr u -- )
  begin  dup  while
    xc@+ xemit
  repeat  2drop ;
```

Iterate with `xc@+`, `xchar+`, `+x/string`. Prefer **`xc!+?`** over unchecked store (returns flag + remaining buffer). Terminal: `xkey`, `xemit`; query `xchar-encoding`.

## Compile-time literals

```forth
[ 2 cells ] literal          \ cell literal
s" tag" sliteral             \ string literal at compile time
```

See `forth-defining.mdc` for `[ … ] literal` and `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc` for Gforth tooling.

## String stack (optional parser aid)

For multi-step parsing without juggling `( c-addr u )` on the data stack, a **string stack** holds counted strings in a dedicated arena (`stringstack` package). Core operations:

| Word | Effect |
|------|--------|
| `"push` | `( c-addr u -- )` push string onto string stack |
| `"pop` | `( -- c-addr u )` pop top string |
| `"th` | `( +n -- c-addr )` nth string from top (0 = TOS) |

Use `"clear` between test cases. **Challenge default:** prefer inline `( c-addr u )` and one buffer unless the task is explicitly a parser pipeline.

## Edit distance (Rosetta Code — verbatim)

`Levenshtein-distance/levenshtein-distance.fth` — **ok** in gforth. Core recursive form (demo on include omitted):

```forth
: levenshtein ( a1 n1 a2 n2 -- n )
  dup
  if
    2>r dup
    if
      2dup 1- chars + c@ 2r@ 1- chars + c@ =
      if  1- 2r> 1- recurse exit
      else
        2dup 1- 2r@ 1- recurse -rot
        2dup    2r@ 1- recurse -rot
        1- 2r> recurse min min 1+
      then
    else  2drop 2r> nip
    then
  else  2drop nip
  then ;
```

Challenge `134-edit-distance.fs` may need iterative DP — do not paste wholesale into paste zones.
